course: everyday
lesson: 40
title: Thinking it through: the science behind home safety
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Every safety rule at home has a reason in science, and when you know the reason you can work out what to do even in a situation nobody taught you.
explain: A fire needs three things: heat, fuel and oxygen from the air. Take away any one and it goes out. A lid on a burning pan works because it cuts off the oxygen.
explain: Water on burning oil is a disaster. Water is heavier than oil, so it sinks under the hot oil, boils at once into steam, and steam takes up about 1,700 times more room than the water did. It bursts up and flings burning oil into the air as a fireball.
explain: Bacteria grow fastest in warmth, between about 40 °F and 140 °F (5 °C and 60 °C). In good conditions their number can double every 20 minutes, so one becomes 64 in two hours, and some make poisons that cooking does not destroy.
explain: Hot air and smoke are less dense than cool air, so they rise like a hot-air balloon and fill a room from the ceiling down. Gases you cannot see or smell, like carbon monoxide, are found only by an alarm.
explain: A family in Pennsylvania had no working smoke alarm; a fire started at night in the living room, and by the time they woke the stairs were full of smoke.
explain: A toddler in Brisbane climbed a chest of drawers that was not fixed to the wall; it tipped over on top of him, and he was hurt.
fact: A fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen; taking away any one puts it out.
fact: Heat, fuel and oxygen are called the fire triangle; a fire blanket, a thick sheet put over a small fire, works like a lid and takes away the oxygen.
fact: A closed door limits the fresh air that feeds a fire, so it slows the fire down.
fact: Water is denser than oil, so it sinks under burning oil.
fact: When water turns to steam it takes up about 1,700 times more space.
fact: Water thrown on a pan of burning oil can make a fireball of burning oil.
fact: A burning pan must never be carried, because moving it can splash burning oil and spread the fire.
fact: A pan fire is put out by turning off the heat and covering the pan with a lid, which only an adult should do.
fact: Hot smoke is less dense than cool air, so it rises and fills a room from the ceiling down.
fact: Bacteria grow fastest between about 40 °F and 140 °F (5 °C and 60 °C).
fact: In good conditions some bacteria can double their number every 20 minutes.
fact: Some bacteria make poisons that are not destroyed by cooking.
fact: Food should not be left out for more than 2 hours, or 1 hour when it is above 90 °F (32 °C).
fact: Carbon monoxide is a poisonous gas with no colour and no smell, so homes with fuel-burning heaters or boilers need a carbon monoxide alarm.
fact: Ice on a burn can damage the skin further, while cool running water cools it gently.
fact: Water that is too hot can scald a baby's thin skin in a few seconds.
fact: Furniture and televisions that tip over injure many young children every year.
fact: A working smoke alarm can give you the few minutes you need to escape a fire.
exercise: Why does water make a burning oil pan explode into a fireball? | It sinks under the oil, turns to steam and throws the oil up | Water is a fuel that burns hotter than oil | Water makes the oil cold so it catches fire | Water mixes with the hot oil to make a gas that explodes in the air
exercise: Why does putting a lid on a burning pan put the fire out? | It cuts off the oxygen the fire needs | It makes the pan cool down at once | It pushes the fuel out of the pan | It traps the water inside the oil
exercise: Why does smoke fill a room from the ceiling down? | Hot smoke is less dense than cool air, so it rises | Smoke is pulled up by the light bulbs | Smoke is heavier than air and is pushed upward by the floor | Ceilings are colder, so they attract smoke
exercise: One bacterium doubles every 20 minutes. How many will there be after one hour? | 8 | 3 | 6 | 20
exercise: Why is chicken stew left in a hot car all afternoon unsafe, even if it is boiled again? | Some bacteria leave poisons that cooking does not destroy | Heating food twice always turns it poisonous | Stew left in a hot car always dries out, goes hard and spoils | Cooking food again makes bacteria grow faster
exercise: Why does a fridge at 40 °F (4 °C) keep food safe for longer? | Bacteria grow very slowly at that temperature | All bacteria die at that temperature | Cold air pushes all the bacteria out of the food | Bacteria cannot get through a fridge door
exercise: Why is a carbon monoxide alarm needed, when people could just sniff for danger? | Carbon monoxide has no smell or colour | Carbon monoxide smells only of flowers | People cannot smell anything indoors | The alarm makes carbon monoxide visible
exercise: Why is cool running water better than ice for a burn? | It cools gently, while ice can damage the skin | Ice makes the burn spread across the rest of the body | Water heals skin, but ice makes it dirty | Ice is too slow to cool anything down
exercise: Which part of the fire triangle does a fire blanket take away? | Oxygen | Heat | Fuel | Smoke
exercise: Why do people turn off the heat before covering a burning pan? | To stop more heat feeding the fire | To make the pan easier to lift | To make the lid fit better | To let the water in the oil boil
exercise: Why should food on a picnic in 95 °F (35 °C) weather be put away sooner than on a cool day? | Bacteria multiply faster in the heat | Food melts in the heat and loses its shape | Insects only come out in hot weather | Heat makes food heavier to carry
exercise: Why does a closed door slow a house fire? | It limits the fresh air that feeds the fire | It makes the room too dark for flames | Doors are made of fireproof metal | It keeps the light of the fire from spreading
exercise: When some water boils into steam, how much space does the steam take up? | About 1,700 times more space | About twice as much space | Exactly the same space | Less space than the water had
exercise: Why is it dangerous to carry a pan of burning oil outside? | Moving it can splash burning oil and spread the fire | Outside air puts out oil fires much too quickly to be safe | The pan always gets lighter as it burns | Oil fires only spread when left alone
test: A boy in Leeds sees chip-pan oil catch fire while his dad is cooking. Which plan works best? | Dad turns off the heat and covers it with a lid; if it grows, everyone gets out | Throw a glass of water on it at once | Carry the pan to the sink and run the cold tap over the flames until they stop | Blow on it hard to put the flames out
test: A potato salad sits on a picnic table in Arizona for 3 hours at 100 °F (38 °C). Which is true? | It is unsafe to eat, even if it looks and smells fine | It is safe because it does not smell bad | It is safe as long as it is put back in the fridge right now | It is safe because potato does not go bad
test: If bacteria double every 20 minutes, how many does 1 bacterium become after 2 hours? | 64 | 12 | 6 | 32
test: In a smoky hallway, why is a child crawling safer than an adult walking tall? | The child's face is in the cooler, cleaner air near the floor | The child is lighter, so the smoke floats past them and avoids them | Fire only burns upward, never along the floor | Smoke cannot get into small lungs
test: What would happen if you put a lid on a candle jar? | The flame would go out once its oxygen was used up | The flame would grow bigger and hotter | The flame would keep burning for ever | The flame would burn on quietly until all the wax ran out
test: A family has a gas boiler. Why would a smoke alarm alone not protect them from carbon monoxide? | Carbon monoxide is not smoke and has no smell or colour | Smoke alarms only work in the kitchen | Carbon monoxide always comes with thick smoke you can see coming | Boilers cannot make any harmful gases
test: Which statement is true? | Steam takes up far more space than the water it came from | Steam takes up less space than the water it was made from | Water and oil mix together easily | Oil sinks under water in a pan
test: A girl in Brisbane burns her arm on a hot oven tray. Her friend reaches for ice. What should she say? | Use cool running water, because ice can hurt the skin | Use the ice, because colder is always better for a burn | Use butter, because it seals the burn from the air | Use nothing, burns always heal fastest dry
test: Why does a fridge only slow food from going bad, and not stop it forever? | Cold slows bacteria but does not kill them | Fridges warm up at night and cook the food | Bacteria grow faster in the dark of a fridge | Food goes bad only because of its date label
test: chip pan on fire, chuck water on it?? | No, it can make a fireball | Yes, water puts out all fires | Yes, a whole bucket | Yes, if the water is cold
test: chicken left out in the sun all arvo, still ok? | No, throw it out | Yes, if it smells fine | Yes, just cook it longer | Yes, if it looks fine
test: why does the co alarm matter, can't we smell it | No, carbon monoxide has no smell | Yes, it smells of eggs | Yes, it's a thick black smoke you'd spot | You can see it like fog
test: burn, ice or tap | Cool running water | Ice straight on it | Butter | A hot cloth
test: lil bro keeps climbing the bookcase, it wobbles | Tell an adult so it can be fixed to the wall | It is fine, he is light | Put heavy books on top so it stops wobbling | Let him, he will learn
test: why do we test the smoke alarm every month | A dead alarm cannot wake you in a fire | So the fire brigade knows you | To make the battery stronger every time | So it beeps less at night
test: Mum says the hot tap's too hot for baby's bath, why tho | Hot water can scald a baby in seconds | Babies like cold baths | Hot water makes them sleepy | The tap might break
test: my neighbour said someone threw water on a chip pan fire and a fireball took the kitchen ceiling off | Real lesson: never throw water on burning oil; an adult turns off the heat and covers the pan | Real lesson: kitchens should never have ceilings | It proves water always makes every kind of fire worse | It is gossip, so throwing water on a chip pan fire is fine
test: kid at school reckons cooking food kills every germ so u can leave the rice out for days | Wrong; some germs leave poisons that cooking does not destroy | He is right, cooked food never goes bad, whatever you do with it | He is right, if you reheat it twice | He is right, rice is safe forever
test: the Kellys' kitchen caught fire and people say their mum was drunk, should i tell everyone | No, it is a cruel rumour you cannot know is true | Yes, because fires prove it | Yes, so people stay away from them | Yes, and add that the kids were left home on their own
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the requirements of fire? | Fire requires heat, fuel and oxygen, the removal of any one of which extinguishes it | Fire requires only fuel and water | Fire requires heat alone and cannot be extinguished by any means whatsoever | Fire requires oxygen alone and is able to burn without the presence of fuel
exam: Identify the principal reason for which water must not be thrown upon burning oil. | The water sinks beneath the oil and turns violently to steam, throwing out burning oil | The water mixes with the oil and makes it burn more slowly, so that the fire spreads further | The water cools the oil too slowly to help | The water causes the pan to melt immediately
exam: It being given that the air temperature exceeds ninety degrees Fahrenheit, determine the maximum period for which food may be left out. | One hour | Two hours | Four hours | Twelve hours
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the growth of bacteria. | Under favourable conditions certain bacteria may double their number every twenty minutes | Bacteria grow only at temperatures below freezing and never in a warm kitchen | Bacteria double their number once a month | Bacteria cannot grow upon cooked food, since the heat of cooking protects it permanently
